, BandMark, Mark, MarkRenderer, Overlay, PaintBindings, PathMark, PointMark, Rect, RectMark, ResolvedTheme, RuleMark, Scene,\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:24:const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:219:  /** Bar patterns, created on first use (svgRects). */\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:227:export const svgPath: SvgMark<PathMark> = (_c, n, mark, bindings) => updatePath(n, mark, bindings);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:229:export const svgArea: SvgMark<AreaMark> = (_c, n, mark, bindings) => {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:235:export const svgRects: SvgMark<RectMark> = (c, n, mark, bindings, _dpr, clip) => {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:245:export const svgPoints: SvgMark<PointMark> = (_c, n, mark, bindings) => {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:250:export const svgArcs: SvgMark<ArcMark> = (c, n, mark, bindings) => updateArcs(c.doc, n, mark, bindings, c.scratch);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:252:export const svgRule: SvgMark<RuleMark> = (c, n, mark, bindings, dpr) => updateRule(n, mark, bindings, dpr, c.scratch);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:254:export const svgBand: SvgMark<BandMark> = (_c, n, mark, bindings) => {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:308:  path: svgPath, area: svgArea, rects: svgRects, points: svgPoints, arcs: svgArcs, rule: svgRule, band: svgBand,\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:311:/** The SVG renderer with every mark handler (a chart kind's composition uses svgRendererWith and its own). */\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:312:export function createSvgRenderer(doc: Document = document): MarkRenderer {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:313:  return svgRendererWith(ALL_MARKS, doc);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:316:export function svgRendererWith(marks: SvgMarks, doc: Document = document): MarkRenderer {\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:317:  const svg = doc.createElementNS(NS, 'svg');\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:318:  svg.setAttribute('class', 'axylo-svg');\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:319:  svg.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:320:  svg.setAttribute('focusable', 'false');\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:324:  ] as const) svg.style.setProperty(k, v);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:341:  svg.append(defs, background.el, sceneGroup, overlayGroup);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:354:    kind: 'svg',\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:355:    element: svg,\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:361:        svg.setAttribute('width', String(width));\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:362:        svg.setAttribute('height', String(height));\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:363:        svg.setAttribute('viewBox', `0 0 ${width} ${height}`);\npackages/axylo/src/render/svg.ts:401:      svg.remove();\n{\n  \"name\": \"axylo-bench\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"description\": \"Benchmark harness: the same workloads through Axylo and comparison libraries (docs/04-plan-performance.md).\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"build\": \"node build.mjs\",\n    \"test\": \"node --test test/*.test.mjs\",\n    \"test:browser\": \"node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/browser/*.test.mjs\",\n    \"bench\": \"node run.mjs\",\n    \"analyze\": \"node analyze.mjs\",\n    \"coverage\": \"node tools/coverage.mjs\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"chart.js\": \"^4.6.1\",\n    \"chartjs-adapter-date-fns\": \"^3.0.0\",\n    \"date-fns\": \"^4.4.0\",\n    \"uplot\": \"^1.6.32\"\n  }\n}\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### 2.1 Pipeline\n\n```\noptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n                                         ├─► summary and table\n                                         └─► focus model\n```\n\nThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n\n| Stage | Output | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n\n### 2.2 Invalidation\n\nStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n\n| Stage | Reruns when |\n|---|---|\n| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n| scene | Layout or view model. |\n| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n\nText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n\n### 2.3 Rendering\n\n**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n\n**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n\nM1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n\n**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\n\n**No animation system.** First render is not animated (I5). Updates cut, or tween for at most 200 ms where the cost is negligible, and not at all under reduced motion.\n\n### 2.4 Document structure\n\n```html\n<figure class=\"axylo\" id=\"ax7\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"ax7-t\" aria-describedby=\"ax7-s\">\n  <figcaption>\n    <h3 id=\"ax7-t\">Daily sessions by channel</h3>        <!-- level from options; or omitted when labelledBy is used -->\n    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n  </figcaption>\n  <div class=\"axylo-plot\" tabindex=\"0\"> … marks … <div class=\"axylo-text\" aria-hidden=\"true\">tick labels</div></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-toolbar\" role=\"toolbar\" aria-label=\"Chart controls\">   <!-- one tab stop, roving focus -->\n    legend toggles · zoom in · zoom out · reset · pause · view as table\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"axylo-note\" role=\"status\">Values not shown: 2. See table.</p>\n  <div class=\"axylo-live\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-tooltip\" hidden></div>\n  <div class=\"axylo-table\" hidden><!-- built when opened: caption, headers, pager --></div>\n</figure>\n```\n\nIds are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n\nDefault styles are applied without injecting a `<style>` element, and a standalone stylesheet ships for pages that prefer to load it themselves. Which mechanism passes a strict Content Security Policy is settled by the M7 test with real headers, not assumed.\n\n### 2.5 Repository\n\n| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1.1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope. (1.1× since 2026-11-18, the owner's decision: once a chart opened on typed columns is appended to, exact x, exact y and one state byte per point are 17 bytes per point against the caller's 16, measured 17,069,760 bytes for 1,000,000 points. Staying under 1× would need segmented columns, a contract change that was not judged worth it for 1.0.) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n\nThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n\n1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n\n## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n\n\"Status\" records what the reviews changed.\n\n| Decision | What decided it | Status after review |\n|---|---|---|\n| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\n| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\n| Accessibility is in the core, on by default | R11–R13. | Kept. The table is built on demand, not eagerly. |\n| The normalized data, not the drawing, is the source for values, table, keyboard and summary | Reviews: a simplified drawing cannot return the values it dropped. | New. |\n| No animation on first render | Reading starts at first paint, and with 24 charts loading at once motion competes with it. R14: four of seven animating libraries ignore reduced motion. | Kept, with the rationale restated. Update transitions stay capped at 200 ms. |\n| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \"everything always fits\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\n| One logical table, three input forms | D2. | Changed from \"one data shape\": typed columns and wide rows are first-class. |\n| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\n| SVG marks with DOM text | The two renderers were built on the same scene and measured. | The owner chose SVG as the only mark renderer for 1.0 on 2026-11-22. See [decision 0002](decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md). |\n| Simplified drawing that preserves extremes, on by default | D8. | Kept for lines and areas, with the guarantee restated per pixel column and per run of valid samples. \"Every spike survives\" was wrong as written. |\n| Palette held to measurable thresholds | R7. | Kept as a screen, marked provisional. Marks carry a non-color cue in every mode, which the first draft did not require. |\n| Colors and fonts from CSS | R10. | Kept. Repaint triggers specified; `palette` option replaced by `colors` keyed by series name. |\n| Numeric strings | R5. | Changed: a whole column of plain decimal strings is parsed with a hint; mixed columns are not. |\n| Chart types | Judgment, then the owner's decision. | All five in 1.0, plus the compact variant (3.7). |\n| MIT, including zoom and accessibility | D7. | Kept. |\n| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Built early to test the core API, reviewed twice, then removed from 1.0 by the owner on 2026-11-18 to keep the first release plain TypeScript. Its last state is at commit dac5664 (`packages/react`), the starting point when adapters for React, Vue and Svelte are added. |\n| Budgets apply to defaults | D8. | Kept. The ratio to uPlot is no longer a gate. |\n| Zero runtime dependencies | D4. | Kept provisionally; time-zone arithmetic uses `Intl`. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
